7592186161987 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161987 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161987 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161987 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Matrix of a Quadratic Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161987 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161987 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "eb93c0ea-26d9-5190-9677-b8c7eb6f7ef1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162219 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162219 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162219 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162219 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162219 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162219 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162219 #edb/ref 1045 "Quadratic Forms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162219 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186162851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162219 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162219 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162219 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b8d01ac6-d0ed-56fb-a724-8bf89dbf7d77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162595 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162595 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93705" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162595 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162595 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162595 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef8e1919-faae-510c-bb46-8c3989805365" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162595 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =1{x}_{1}{x}_{1}+(-2){x}_{1}{x}_{2}+(-2){x}_{2}{x}_{1}+1{x}_{2}{x}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})+{x}_{2}(-2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162597 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162597 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162597 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162597 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ b & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}\\,$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162597 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cedbc16a-539c-525b-a8a8-cb2cc644cfec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162597 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =1{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & +\\,4{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+1{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must split the coefficient of $8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ between the entries \n\n$$\n{a}_{12}={a}_{21}=\\frac{8}{2}=4\n$$\n\nof our matrix. So, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1$ and $b=4.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b=1+4=5.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162616 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162616 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94065" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162616 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162616 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,K=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162616 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6fea8541-6770-5397-b251-59a7fad8f7ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162616 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ has an associated $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $H$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $J$ could also be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $K$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}.$ Although it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix, $K$ is not symmetric since ${k}_{23}\\ne {k}_{32}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$H$ and $J$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162851 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162851 #edb/ref 1061 30535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162851 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "79f6f6cd-00ca-545a-aec0-257cfc16bf2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162851 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162866 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162866 #edb/ref 1061 30584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162866 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c50bbdb7-ec08-57b4-bc96-b8e668bc34db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162866 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162867 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162867 #edb/ref 1061 30585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162867 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "193a5105-f949-5b8b-a134-e0f453f3f2c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162867 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162868 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162868 #edb/ref 1061 30586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162868 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "62f6605e-8903-5f4e-b4de-3b766aac20b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162868 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162869 #edb/ref 1061 30587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162869 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f1a187e7-1f2b-511d-b52d-f35fecd8236e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162869 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163226 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a bilinear form $B(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})$ such that \n\n$$\nB:\\Bbb R^n \\times \\Bbb R^n \\to \\Bbb R\n$$\n\nand the corresponding $n \\times n$ matrix is *symmetric*, we can define the so-called **quadratic form** by substituting $\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})= B(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{x}).\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the new expression depends only on $\\mathbf{x}.$ So, a quadratic form is a function \n\n$$\nQ:\\Bbb R^n \\to \\Bbb R.\n$$\n\nIn matrix notation, a quadratic form  can be given as \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $A$ is a symmetric matrix. It's called the **matrix of the quadratic form**. \n\n**Watch out!** By definition, the matrix of a quadratic form must be *symmetric*.\n\nFor example, given the bilinear form \n\n![](images/99/996c4aa136dd7cda56787a2edb0f9481ed3d9356248a27afcde050ca613b6b9c.svg) \n\nthe corresponding quadratic form is then\n$Q( \\mathbf{x})=B( \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{x}).$\nExplicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n**Note**: In a quadratic form, all terms must be of degree two (considered as polynomials in the variables $x_1, x_2, \\cdots, x_n$). That's the reason we call it *quadratic*." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163226 #edb/ref 1065 4884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163226 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f35d3e6c-6386-5c92-91c9-05fe3d888a47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163226 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269297 #edb/ref 1080 "21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269297 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269297 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03315f4c-29f9-5335-bf32-1a4c56087564" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269352 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269352 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269352 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09186c66-3df7-5958-bab9-d6eceedcf24b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269498 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269498 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269498 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1bc9401d-5750-5d84-973f-2999e18b0e40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269883 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269883 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269883 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "43ef2164-