7592186270103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271655 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271655 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271655 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186270103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271655 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4fa92d2a-c490-5653-aff1-56f027732c5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271697 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271697 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271697 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271697 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271697 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271697 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271697 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271697 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186269297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271697 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "685add4a-af6c-5961-9669-2663c3076fb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272168 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465006 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272168 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465007 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272168 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465008 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272168 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140870" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272168 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272168 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,T=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272168 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d68a509c-d790-5483-ab3b-63aef29c3b19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272168 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ has an associated $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $R$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix (not $3\\times 3$).\n\n- $S$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}.$ Although it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix, $S$ is not symmetric since ${s}_{23}\\ne {s}_{32}.$\n\n- $T$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272171 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272171 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140928" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272171 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272171 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272171 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d5a533cc-6fd5-51dd-a1d8-7511844d6151" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272171 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =1{x}_{1}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+(-1){x}_{1}{x}_{3}+0{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+(-1){x}_{3}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}{x}_{2}+0{x}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{3}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}{x}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}({x}_{1}-{x}_{3})+{x}_{2}(3{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}(-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}) \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{3}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}{x}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272172 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272172 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140929" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272172 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272172 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the quadratic form \n$Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-3{x}_{2}{x}_{3}.$ \nEvaluate $Q(x)$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272172 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "95eb1ba0-88cc-5104-bc0d-3a93ab6de4cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272172 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given that \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the expression for $Q(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-3{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-4\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 3\\cdot 0-3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =9+12+0+0 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272173 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272173 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140932" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272173 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272173 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ b & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}\\,$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272173 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bcdcd1b6-5a07-5e6a-b243-0a76c6534f4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272173 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+(-2){x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & +\\,(-2){x}_{2}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must split the coefficient of $-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ between the entries \n\n$$\n{a}_{12}={a}_{21}=\\frac{(-4)}{2}=-2\n$$\n\nof our matrix. So, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2$ and $b=-2.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b=2+(-2)=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354673 #edb/ref 1080 "2{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354673 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354673 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "021bd2b7-706e-57b3-a8af-b3a83db7afce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354679 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354679 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354679 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "02a9e9a8-3c46-5c6f-a0b9-c715263b46fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354727 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{3}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354727 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354727 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "074bc0a8-4ace-5935-8132-e8516c14bc11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354758 #edb/ref 1080 "55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354758 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354758 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0a4453eb-2267-5b35-a0fa-3451bada2711" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354776 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}"